1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,355
<font color="#FFFFFF">Vision transformers are powerful but data-hungry:
they only beat convolutional networks</font>

2
00:00:06,435 --> 00:00:09,152
<font color="#FFFFFF">when huge labeled datasets are available.</font>

3
00:00:09,232 --> 00:00:11,267
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper introduces PatchRot,</font>

4
00:00:11,347 --> 00:00:16,247
<font color="#FFFFFF">a self-supervised technique crafted specifically
for vision transformers.</font>

5
00:00:16,327 --> 00:00:17,816
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key idea is simple.</font>

6
00:00:17,896 --> 00:00:23,274
<font color="#FFFFFF">Rotate the whole image or its individual patches by
multiples of ninety degrees,</font>

7
00:00:23,354 --> 00:00:27,231
<font color="#FFFFFF">and train the transformer to predict every rotation angle.</font>

8
00:00:27,311 --> 00:00:32,143
<font color="#FFFFFF">The class token predicts the image rotation,
capturing global structure,</font>

9
00:00:32,223 --> 00:00:37,600
<font color="#FFFFFF">while new per-patch heads predict each patch's
rotation, capturing local detail.</font>

10
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:39,169
<font color="#FFFFFF">After this pretraining,</font>

11
00:00:39,249 --> 00:00:46,947
<font color="#FFFFFF">PatchRot features consistently beat both supervised
training from scratch and the RotNet baseline across CIFAR-10,</font>

12
00:00:47,027 --> 00:00:49,880
<font color="#FFFFFF">CIFAR-100, FashionMNIST, and Tiny-ImageNet.</font>

13
00:00:50,260 --> 00:00:55,250
<font color="#FFFFFF">Vision transformers have overtaken convolutional
networks on many vision tasks,</font>

14
00:00:55,330 --> 00:00:58,652
<font color="#FFFFFF">but only when trained on very large labeled datasets.</font>

15
00:00:58,732 --> 00:01:02,503
<font color="#FFFFFF">With limited labels their performance falls behind
ConvNets,</font>

16
00:01:02,583 --> 00:01:08,087
<font color="#FFFFFF">because they lack built-in inductive biases like
locality and translation equivariance.</font>

17
00:01:08,167 --> 00:01:11,873
<font color="#FFFFFF">Labeling data at the scale ViTs need is expensive
and slow.</font>

18
00:01:11,953 --> 00:01:16,815
<font color="#FFFFFF">Self-supervised learning can help by learning
useful features without labels,</font>

19
00:01:16,895 --> 00:01:22,591
<font color="#FFFFFF">but the popular self-supervised pretext tasks were
all designed for convolutional networks</font>

20
00:01:22,671 --> 00:01:27,020
<font color="#FFFFFF">and ignore the patch-token structure that makes
transformers special.</font>

21
00:01:27,400 --> 00:01:32,060
<font color="#FFFFFF">A vision transformer splits an image into patches
and applies self-attention,</font>

22
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:37,170
<font color="#FFFFFF">so unlike a convolutional network it can produce a
separate output for every patch,</font>

23
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:39,632
<font color="#FFFFFF">not just one output for the whole image.</font>

24
00:01:39,712 --> 00:01:44,865
<font color="#FFFFFF">Prior work called RotNet showed that simply
predicting the rotation angle of an image</font>

25
00:01:44,945 --> 00:01:48,497
<font color="#FFFFFF">teaches a convolutional network surprisingly rich
features.</font>

26
00:01:48,577 --> 00:01:53,483
<font color="#FFFFFF">The natural question is: can we push rotation
prediction down to the patch level,</font>

27
00:01:53,563 --> 00:01:59,885
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the transformer learns local features for each
patch as well as global structure for the whole image?</font>

28
00:01:59,965 --> 00:02:04,810
<font color="#FFFFFF">That patch-level signal is exactly what a
token-based model is built to exploit.</font>

29
00:02:05,190 --> 00:02:08,836
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper makes three main contributions.</font>

30
00:02:08,916 --> 00:02:12,562
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, it introduces PatchRot,</font>

31
00:02:12,642 --> 00:02:16,287
<font color="#FFFFFF">a self-supervised technique crafted for vision
transformers that predicts rotation angles</font>

32
00:02:16,367 --> 00:02:20,013
<font color="#FFFFFF">at two levels: the class token predicts the
whole-image rotation for global context,</font>

33
00:02:20,093 --> 00:02:23,739
<font color="#FFFFFF">and new per-patch heads predict each patch's
rotation for local detail.</font>

34
00:02:23,819 --> 00:02:27,465
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

35
00:02:27,545 --> 00:02:31,191
<font color="#FFFFFF">it introduces a buffer gap between patches during
training so the network cannot cheat by matching continuous edges,</font>

36
00:02:31,271 --> 00:02:34,917
<font color="#FFFFFF">forcing it to learn genuine content.</font>

37
00:02:34,997 --> 00:02:38,642
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

38
00:02:38,722 --> 00:02:42,368
<font color="#FFFFFF">it demonstrates through extensive experiments that
PatchRot beats both supervised training</font>

39
00:02:42,448 --> 00:02:46,094
<font color="#FFFFFF">from scratch and the RotNet baseline across
multiple datasets,</font>

40
00:02:46,174 --> 00:02:49,820
<font color="#FFFFFF">and that its features transfer well and help in
semi-supervised settings.</font>

41
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:51,767
<font color="#FFFFFF">PatchRot works as follows.</font>

42
00:02:51,847 --> 00:02:55,189
<font color="#FFFFFF">Take an input image and either rotate the whole image,</font>

43
00:02:55,269 --> 00:02:57,977
<font color="#FFFFFF">or rotate each of its patches independently,</font>

44
00:02:58,057 --> 00:03:03,109
<font color="#FFFFFF">by a random multiple of ninety degrees: zero,
ninety, one-eighty, or two-seventy.</font>

45
00:03:03,189 --> 00:03:09,953
<font color="#FFFFFF">The vision transformer is then trained to predict
these rotation angles as a simple four-way classification.</font>

46
00:03:10,033 --> 00:03:14,705
<font color="#FFFFFF">The transformer's class token, which normally
predicts the object category,</font>

47
00:03:14,785 --> 00:03:19,647
<font color="#FFFFFF">is repurposed to predict the whole-image rotation,
capturing global structure.</font>

48
00:03:19,727 --> 00:03:26,554
<font color="#FFFFFF">New multilayer-perceptron heads are attached to
each patch token to predict that individual patch's rotation,</font>

49
00:03:26,634 --> 00:03:28,011
<font color="#FFFFFF">capturing local detail.</font>

50
00:03:28,091 --> 00:03:30,355
<font color="#FFFFFF">To prevent the network from cheating,</font>

51
00:03:30,435 --> 00:03:34,664
<font color="#FFFFFF">patches are carved from a slightly larger grid and
randomly cropped,</font>

52
00:03:34,744 --> 00:03:40,240
<font color="#FFFFFF">so a random buffer gap sits between them and edge
continuity can't give away the answer.</font>

53
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:42,838
<font color="#FFFFFF">Training happens at a reduced resolution;</font>

54
00:03:42,918 --> 00:03:48,350
<font color="#FFFFFF">then the extra patch heads are removed and the
network is fine-tuned at full resolution</font>

55
00:03:48,430 --> 00:03:50,378
<font color="#FFFFFF">on the real classification task,</font>

56
00:03:50,458 --> 00:03:54,560
<font color="#FFFFFF">with positional embeddings interpolated to the
larger patch count.</font>

57
00:03:54,940 --> 00:04:00,539
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors test PatchRot on four standard image
classification datasets: CIFAR-10,</font>

58
00:04:00,619 --> 00:04:04,850
<font color="#FFFFFF">CIFAR-100, FashionMNIST at thirty-two by thirty-two
resolution,</font>

59
00:04:04,930 --> 00:04:07,998
<font color="#FFFFFF">and Tiny-ImageNet at sixty-four by sixty-four.</font>

60
00:04:08,078 --> 00:04:13,404
<font color="#FFFFFF">They also study SVHN and MNIST to probe
rotation-invariant objects like digits.</font>

61
00:04:13,484 --> 00:04:18,125
<font color="#FFFFFF">The backbone is a compact vision transformer with
six encoder blocks.</font>

62
00:04:18,205 --> 00:04:23,531
<font color="#FFFFFF">Patch sizes are four pixels for the small datasets
and eight for Tiny-ImageNet,</font>

63
00:04:23,611 --> 00:04:27,158
<font color="#FFFFFF">with a buffer gap set to a quarter of the patch size.</font>

64
00:04:27,238 --> 00:04:29,074
<font color="#FFFFFF">Beyond plain classification,</font>

65
00:04:29,154 --> 00:04:33,384
<font color="#FFFFFF">they evaluate transfer learning between CIFAR-10
and CIFAR-100,</font>

66
00:04:33,464 --> 00:04:38,380
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a semi-supervised setting on CIFAR-10 where
only a handful of labels,</font>

67
00:04:38,460 --> 00:04:42,280
<font color="#FFFFFF">from two hundred fifty up to ten thousand, are available.</font>

68
00:04:42,660 --> 00:04:48,348
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline result is that PatchRot pretraining
consistently outperforms both supervised</font>

69
00:04:48,428 --> 00:04:51,913
<font color="#FFFFFF">training from scratch and the RotNet rotation baseline,</font>

70
00:04:51,993 --> 00:04:53,468
<font color="#FFFFFF">on every dataset tested.</font>

71
00:04:53,548 --> 00:04:55,672
<font color="#FFFFFF">On CIFAR-10 with full fine-tuning,</font>

72
00:04:55,752 --> 00:04:59,755
<font color="#FFFFFF">PatchRot reaches ninety-two point six percent
top-one accuracy,</font>

73
00:04:59,835 --> 00:05:05,005
<font color="#FFFFFF">compared to eighty-three point nine percent for
supervised training from scratch.</font>

74
00:05:05,085 --> 00:05:12,329
<font color="#FFFFFF">On CIFAR-100 the gap is even larger: seventy point
six percent top-one versus fifty point two percent supervised,</font>

75
00:05:12,409 --> 00:05:14,792
<font color="#FFFFFF">and ninety point two percent top-five.</font>

76
00:05:14,872 --> 00:05:20,041
<font color="#FFFFFF">On FashionMNIST it improves ninety-four point one
versus eighty-nine point eight.</font>

77
00:05:20,121 --> 00:05:22,115
<font color="#FFFFFF">Remarkably, even linear probing,</font>

78
00:05:22,195 --> 00:05:26,717
<font color="#FFFFFF">where the entire network is frozen and only the
final layer is trained,</font>

79
00:05:26,797 --> 00:05:29,115
<font color="#FFFFFF">gets close to supervised performance,</font>

80
00:05:29,195 --> 00:05:34,300
<font color="#FFFFFF">and fine-tuning just a single encoder block already
beats training from scratch.</font>

81
00:05:34,680 --> 00:05:39,885
<font color="#FFFFFF">The ablation study on CIFAR-10 confirms that every
component of PatchRot matters.</font>

82
00:05:39,965 --> 00:05:44,060
<font color="#FFFFFF">Training on patch rotations alone, without
whole-image rotation,</font>

83
00:05:44,140 --> 00:05:49,801
<font color="#FFFFFF">drops accuracy to ninety-one point eight percent
because the model loses global context.</font>

84
00:05:49,881 --> 00:05:55,282
<font color="#FFFFFF">Using only image rotation, essentially the RotNet
approach adapted to a transformer,</font>

85
00:05:55,362 --> 00:05:57,500
<font color="#FFFFFF">drops it to ninety-one point zero.</font>

86
00:05:57,580 --> 00:06:01,479
<font color="#FFFFFF">Rotating the image and its patches together in a
single pass,</font>

87
00:06:01,559 --> 00:06:04,742
<font color="#FFFFFF">rather than in separate passes, hurts performance,</font>

88
00:06:04,822 --> 00:06:09,830
<font color="#FFFFFF">as does training at the original resolution instead
of the reduced resolution,</font>

89
00:06:09,910 --> 00:06:14,397
<font color="#FFFFFF">and reusing the existing head instead of adding
dedicated patch heads.</font>

90
00:06:14,477 --> 00:06:19,160
<font color="#FFFFFF">Each of these lands below the full method's
ninety-two point six percent,</font>

91
00:06:19,240 --> 00:06:21,900
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing every design decision contributes.</font>

92
00:06:22,280 --> 00:06:24,237
<font color="#FFFFFF">The numbers tell a clear story.</font>

93
00:06:24,317 --> 00:06:29,232
<font color="#FFFFFF">On CIFAR-10, PatchRot reaches ninety-two point six
percent top-one accuracy,</font>

94
00:06:29,312 --> 00:06:34,555
<font color="#FFFFFF">an improvement of eight point seven points over
supervised training from scratch.</font>

95
00:06:34,635 --> 00:06:37,907
<font color="#FFFFFF">On CIFAR-100 the improvement is over twenty points,</font>

96
00:06:37,987 --> 00:06:41,718
<font color="#FFFFFF">from fifty point two to seventy point six percent top-one,</font>

97
00:06:41,798 --> 00:06:44,216
<font color="#FFFFFF">and ninety point two percent top-five.</font>

98
00:06:44,296 --> 00:06:50,064
<font color="#FFFFFF">FashionMNIST improves to ninety-four point one
percent and Tiny-ImageNet's top-five rises</font>

99
00:06:50,144 --> 00:06:52,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">to seventy-three point four percent.</font>

100
00:06:52,510 --> 00:06:57,622
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the semi-supervised setting with only four
thousand labeled CIFAR-10 images,</font>

101
00:06:57,702 --> 00:07:00,579
<font color="#FFFFFF">PatchRot reaches eighty-one percent accuracy,</font>

102
00:07:00,659 --> 00:07:05,640
<font color="#FFFFFF">versus roughly fifty-four percent for supervised
training on the same labels,</font>

103
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:10,700
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing how valuable the self-supervised features
are when labels are scarce.</font>

104
00:07:11,080 --> 00:07:13,489
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is that a very simple idea,</font>

105
00:07:13,569 --> 00:07:18,094
<font color="#FFFFFF">predicting the rotation of both the whole image and
each individual patch,</font>

106
00:07:18,174 --> 00:07:23,010
<font color="#FFFFFF">turns out to be a self-supervised task perfectly
suited to vision transformers.</font>

107
00:07:23,090 --> 00:07:28,423
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because the class token learns global structure and
the patch heads learn local detail,</font>

108
00:07:28,503 --> 00:07:32,530
<font color="#FFFFFF">PatchRot teaches the transformer rich features
without any labels,</font>

109
00:07:32,610 --> 00:07:39,437
<font color="#FFFFFF">and those features reliably beat supervised
training from scratch and the RotNet baseline across four datasets,</font>

110
00:07:39,517 --> 00:07:43,482
<font color="#FFFFFF">in transfer learning, and especially when labeled
data is scarce.</font>

111
00:07:43,562 --> 00:07:44,726
<font color="#FFFFFF">It is a lightweight,</font>

112
00:07:44,806 --> 00:07:49,020
<font color="#FFFFFF">practical recipe for pretraining vision
transformers on limited data.</font>
